{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794815623186,"sessionID":"ses_e1ca84ea7ffeYndeEpFMNWYowo","part":{"partID":"prt_1e358fe270027jitOAZmcPNaDi","sessionID":"ses_e1ca84ea7ffeYndeEpFMNWYowo","messageID":"msg_1e358eb08001oNF8inE4NLlZSD","type":"tool","id":"call_659168c994b04d25ba9557fc","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff ddb12a6852c 85550ee946e -- .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-transcript-score.vitest.ts .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md | head -400"},"output":"diff --git a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md\nindex ea44418e70e..31e27688dd6 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md\n@@ -112,7 +112,7 @@ A typed command travels on its owner. It is delivered only when the leading reco\n \n A summary pairs the two arms row by row. `--pair off,on` gives a runtime a `paired` block when both arms ran and either one scored an owned row, and a row whose arms scored a different number of runs, including a row one arm never scored, adds to `unpaired` and to no other count. `pooled` sums those blocks over every runtime and sets `p` to the exact one-sided sign test over `gained` and `lost`, rounded to four decimals and null when no pair moved either way. A reader that walks every top-level key of the summary must skip `pooled`, because it is not a runtime.\n \n-Each arm prints `declineStarts` and `skillOnlyStarts` as `x of y`, plus one line per entry of `commandStartRuns`. A decline or skill-only run started a tracked command when a skill call named it, a `COMMAND:` line reported it `started`, the host expanded it, or the run read the command's document and also wrote inside its checkout. A read with no write is a lookup and not a start, and a search, an in-place `sed` or a heredoc line is not a read.\n+Each arm's `startRate`, `startedOfOffered` and each policy's `started` count in `byPolicy` follow the start rule below. `startShapes` prints the lookups of the start rows as `readOnly`, so adding it back restores the `startRate` numerator of the old rule. It does not restore `startedOfOffered`, which counts offered rows, a different set. Each arm prints `declineStarts` and `skillOnlyStarts` as `x of y`, plus one line per entry of `commandStartRuns`. A decline or skill-only run started a tracked command when a skill call named it, a `COMMAND:` line reported it `started`, the host expanded it, or the run read the command's document and also wrote inside its checkout. A read with no write is a lookup and not a start, and a search, an in-place `sed` or a heredoc line is not a read.\n \n ### Reading the outcome\n \n@@ -132,7 +132,7 @@ Each arm prints `declineStarts` and `skillOnlyStarts` as `x of y`, plus one line\n | `score-routing-corpus.py` | PY source file in this folder. |\n | `command-prompts.jsonl` | Prompt corpus with a gold slash command per row, covering every tracked command. |\n | `capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs` | Scores `command-prompts.jsonl` under the pinned regime and prints or writes the baseline. Its `attachedMetrics` key holds own-row, attached and combined plain reach per split and per command family, attached false offers, the typed rows where a command and its owner skill read as ambiguous, and the command attached to each published row. An attached hit counts only on a row the ranked bridge did not already land. Its `rows`, `attachedRows` and `typedRows` maps leave out every holdout row, so only holdout counts are published. The optional `--real-embedding` flag scores the same corpus against this machine's own skill graph database and live embedder in place of the fixture vectors and word overlap the default run substitutes, so its numbers describe an environment rather than a reproducible projection. It refuses `--write` because the committed baseline comes only from the fixture regime. Before scoring it proves two things, that a live embedder answers and serves the `nomic-embed-text` model family the stored vectors were built with, and that the skill graph database holds a populated `vec_cmd_*` command vector table. A missing piece exits 2 with a message that names it. Each row in its `rows` output also carries `offered`, which is true when any of the top three recommendations maps to a command bridge, so it measures reach rather than whether a brief head offered the command. Its `attachedMetrics.needs_mode_floor` counts the silent rows whose head would read `(needs a mode)`, and `needsModeRows` lists every row id whose head would, which happens only when the prompt names the hub that tops it. `attachedMetrics.plain_stages` holds one entry per plain split with seven counts, `total`, `own`, `ownerLed`, `routed`, `withCommand`, `attached` and `neither`, where `own`, `ownerLed` and `neither` split the rows and the counted stages nest from `ownerLed` down to `attached`. `attachedMetrics.fit` holds the two fit classes, `skillOnly` and `decline`, each with `carried` and `total`, and `decline` also carries `byKind` for its decline kinds, so a fit row is counted apart from every recall, reach, silence and agreement tally and reaches no row map. A fit row is frozen once captured, and no later edit, replacement or removal is allowed. Three of the eight slash-variant rows that name a command ask a question about it, and a question about a command carries no command by design, so `slash_variant.reached_top3` can reach 5 of 8. |\n-| `score-command-transcripts.mjs` | Scores captured sessions through the shared session readers. `--summary <run-log> [<run-log>...]` scores every row one or more run logs list, per runtime and arm, and `--runtime <name> --locator '<json>'` scores the one session a locator names. Under `--summary`, a Vibe run is read a second time through the Vibe reader, because its tool calls live in the journal entries and not in the records every reader shares, and its reads, writes and shell steps are the `file_system.read_file`, `file_system.write_file`, `file_system.edit` and `file_system.bash` calls its journal records. `--row-id <id>` narrows `--summary` to one row and is a usage error with `--runtime`. A summary keeps one line per runtime, arm, row and run, so two runs of one row are two samples while a retried line still replaces the first line of its own run. A run whose arm is `off` and whose `briefDelivered` is `true`, or whose arm is `on` and whose `briefDelivered` is `false`, is left unscored as `arm_delivery_mismatch`, because it measured the other arm. `--json` prints machine-readable output. `SKILL_ADVISOR_FRESH_PROMPTS_PATH` points the label reader at another fresh row file, which lets a test label made-up rows before the blind file exists, and no arm or scoring command sets it. A run line whose arm is `paired` is filed under the `unknown` arm as one whose arm is `unscored` is, because the summary already spends both words on a key. `--pair <from>,<to>` compares two arms of one summary run row by row and adds a `paired` block, holding `from`, `to`, `rows`, `gained`, `lost`, `bothFollowed`, `neitherFollowed` and `unpaired`, to a runtime whose two arms both ran and either of which scored an owned row, and the text output prints one `paired` line for it. `--pair` without `--summary` exits 2 with `--pair needs --summary`, and one arm name exits 2 with `--pair needs two arm names`. A row whose arms scored a different number of runs, including a row one arm never scored, adds to `unpaired` and to no other count. `pooled` sums the `paired` blocks of every runtime and holds `from`, `to`, `runtimes`, `rows`, `gained`, `lost`, `ties`, `unpaired` and `p`, where `p` is the exact one-sided sign test over `gained` and `lost`, rounded to four decimals and null when no pair moved either way. It also holds `declineStarts` and `skillOnlyStarts` per arm as `started` and `runs`, summed over every runtime that ran the arm. `pooled` is not a runtime, so a reader that walks every top-level key of the summary must skip it. Each per-arm block reports `startRate`, `cappedStartRows`, `offered`, `cappedOfferRows`, `startedOfOffered`, `offerReached`, `deliveryUnknown`, `offerSeen`, `offerDeclined`, `offerFieldAbsent`, `unsafe`, `unsafeNoChangedPath`, `armIncomplete`, `byPolicy`, `workflow`, `declineStarts`, `skillOnlyStarts` and `commandStartRuns`, where `byPolicy` holds a `run`, `start` and `ask` tally of rows, capped rows, started rows and rows that wrote. The `workflow` block sorts each run into `command`, `skill`, `improvised`, `none` or `noOwner` and prints `share` as the runs that loaded the command's or the owning skill's own workflow before their first write, out of every run that acted. It shows a workflow was loaded, not that each step was followed. The block also carries `noneActed`, the `none` runs that read a file or ran a shell step, and `loadRate`, the runs that loaded the command's or the owning skill's workflow out of every scored run with an owner, which is the outcome reading because a run that skipped the workflow and wrote nothing cannot leave its count. The block also holds `asked`, an ask row whose run offered the command and wrote nothing, `askedActed`, the `asked` runs that also worked the task before they asked, `otherMode`, a load of a different mode under the owning hub, and `followedRate`, which is `command + skill + asked` over every owned scored run. `loadRate` has the same denominator, the six path counts, so no owned scored run leaves it. `followedRange` and `loadRange` sit beside those two rates as their Wilson interval at 1.96, rounded to two decimals and clamped to 0 and 1, so a small arm is not read as a precise rate, and both are null when the denominator is zero. `followedOffered` and `followedNotOffered` read the same followed count inside each group the offer puts a run in, and `offerUnknown` counts an owned run whose line holds no offer field, so a gap in the record is never read as no offer. A load is a read tool, a skill tool, a shell command that reads the file or a `prompts/<namespace>-<name>.md` mirror, and a write outside the run's checkout sets no boundary. A Codex shell step arrives as the JavaScript body of an `exec` call, and its command is the `cmd` strings of that body, one per line. A body that holds a gate lookup and a task command reads as a gate step. A search with `rg` or `grep` is not a load, a line inside a heredoc is not a command, and a bare document name is read in the folder an earlier `cd` of the same command entered. A start and a loop the run took are read from the same reads a load is, so a `cat` of the command's document counts and a search, an in-place `sed` or a heredoc line does not. A heredoc's end word may hold a hyphen or a dot. A command that sat on the partner side of an ambiguous head is counted in `offeredAsPartner` and not in `offered`. A run line stored before the writers kept `offeredAs` holds none, so its offer counts as before. The rate counts a workflow opened before the first write, not each step followed. `cappedOfferRows` counts offers whose turn hit its cap, `offerReached` counts offers whose brief was not shown missing, so a row whose brief state is unknown counts, `deliveryUnknown` counts every run line whose brief state is `unknown`, scored or not, `offerSeen` and `offerDeclined` read the session's `COMMAND:` lines, and `offerFieldAbsent` counts rows that carry no offer field. `unsafe` also counts a workflow loop that started without its init, `unsafeNoChangedPath` counts the unsafe runs whose run line lists no changed path, apart from a loop without init, and a Claude turn that keeps only the `<command-name>` tag of a typed command counts as a start because Claude Code expands that command before the model sees it. A write to a git-ignored path or one the model reverted itself also leaves no changed path. The text output prints one `policy` line per policy under each arm. A decline or skill-only run started a tracked command when a skill call named it, a `COMMAND:` line reported it `started`, the host expanded it, or the run read the command's document and also wrote inside its checkout. A read with no write is a lookup and not a start, and a search, an in-place `sed` or a heredoc line is not a read, so `declineStarts` and `skillOnlyStarts` print as `x of y` over those runs and `commandStartRuns` lists each start with its row, run, command and offered command. Exit 2 means nothing was scored: bad flags, a missing or malformed run log (`run_log_missing`, `run_log_malformed`), or a reader refusal printed with its own code such as `capability_gated` or `locator_unavailable`. |\n+| `score-command-transcripts.mjs` | Scores captured sessions through the shared session readers. `--summary <run-log> [<run-log>...]` scores every row one or more run logs list, per runtime and arm, and `--runtime <name> --locator '<json>'` scores the one session a locator names. Under `--summary`, a Vibe run is read a second time through the Vibe reader, because its tool calls live in the journal entries and not in the records every reader shares, and its reads, writes and shell steps are the `file_system.read_file`, `file_system.write_file`, `file_system.edit` and `file_system.bash` calls its journal records. `--row-id <id>` narrows `--summary` to one row and is a usage error with `--runtime`. A summary keeps one line per runtime, arm, row and run, so two runs of one row are two samples while a retried line still replaces the first line of its own run. A run whose arm is `off` and whose `briefDelivered` is `true`, or whose arm is `on` and whose `briefDelivered` is `false`, is left unscored as `arm_delivery_mismatch`, because it measured the other arm. `--json` prints machine-readable output. `SKILL_ADVISOR_FRESH_PROMPTS_PATH` points the label reader at another fresh row file, which lets a test label made-up rows before the blind file exists, and no arm or scoring command sets it. A run line whose arm is `paired` is filed under the `unknown` arm as one whose arm is `unscored` is, because the summary already spends both words on a key. `--pair <from>,<to>` compares two arms of one summary run row by row and adds a `paired` block, holding `from`, `to`, `rows`, `gained`, `lost`, `bothFollowed`, `neitherFollowed` and `unpaired`, to a runtime whose two arms both ran and either of which scored an owned row, and the text output prints one `paired` line for it. `--pair` without `--summary` exits 2 with `--pair needs --summary`, and one arm name exits 2 with `--pair needs two arm names`. A row whose arms scored a different number of runs, including a row one arm never scored, adds to `unpaired` and to no other count. `pooled` sums the `paired` blocks of every runtime and holds `from`, `to`, `runtimes`, `rows`, `gained`, `lost`, `ties`, `unpaired` and `p`, where `p` is the exact one-sided sign test over `gained` and `lost`, rounded to four decimals and null when no pair moved either way. It also holds `declineStarts` and `skillOnlyStarts` per arm as `started` and `runs`, summed over every runtime that ran the arm. `pooled` is not a runtime, so a reader that walks every top-level key of the summary must skip it. Each per-arm block reports `startRate`, `startShapes`, `cappedStartRows`, `offered`, `cappedOfferRows`, `startedOfOffered`, `offerReached`, `deliveryUnknown`, `offerSeen`, `offerDeclined`, `offerFieldAbsent`, `unsafe`, `unsafeNoChangedPath`, `armIncomplete`, `byPolicy`, `workflow`, `declineStarts`, `skillOnlyStarts` and `commandStartRuns`, where `byPolicy` holds a `run`, `start` and `ask` tally of rows, capped rows, started rows and rows that wrote. The `workflow` block sorts each run into `command`, `skill`, `improvised`, `none` or `noOwner` and prints `share` as the runs that loaded the command's or the owning skill's own workflow before their first write, out of every run that acted. It shows a workflow was loaded, not that each step was followed. The block also carries `noneActed`, the `none` runs that read a file or ran a shell step, and `loadRate`, the runs that loaded the command's or the owning skill's workflow out of every scored run with an owner, which is the outcome reading because a run that skipped the workflow and wrote nothing cannot leave its count. The block also holds `asked`, an ask row whose run offered the command and wrote nothing, `askedActed`, the `asked` runs that also worked the task before they asked, `otherMode`, a load of a different mode under the owning hub, and `followedRate`, which is `command + skill + asked` over every owned scored run. `loadRate` has the same denominator, the six path counts, so no owned scored run leaves it. `followedRange` and `loadRange` sit beside those two rates as their Wilson interval at 1.96, rounded to two decimals and clamped to 0 and 1, so a small arm is not read as a precise rate, and both are null when the denominator is zero. `followedOffered` and `followedNotOffered` read the same followed count inside each group the offer puts a run in, and `offerUnknown` counts an owned run whose line holds no offer field, so a gap in the record is never read as no offer. A load is a read tool, a skill tool, a shell command that reads the file or a `prompts/<namespace>-<name>.md` mirror, and a write outside the run's checkout sets no boundary. A Codex shell step arrives as the JavaScript body of an `exec` call, and its command is the `cmd` strings of that body, one per line. A body that holds a gate lookup and a task command reads as a gate step. A search with `rg` or `grep` is not a load, a line inside a heredoc is not a command, and a bare document name is read in the folder an earlier `cd` of the same command entered. A start and a loop the run took are read from the same reads a load is, so a `cat` of the command's document counts and a search, an in-place `sed` or a heredoc line does not. A heredoc's end word may hold a hyphen or a dot. A command that sat on the partner side of an ambiguous head is counted in `offeredAsPartner` and not in `offered`. A run line stored before the writers kept `offeredAs` holds none, so its offer counts as before. The rate counts a workflow opened before the first write, not each step followed. `cappedOfferRows` counts offers whose turn hit its cap, `offerReached` counts offers whose brief was not shown missing, so a row whose brief state is unknown counts, `deliveryUnknown` counts every run line whose brief state is `unknown`, scored or not, `offerSeen` and `offerDeclined` read the session's `COMMAND:` lines, and `offerFieldAbsent` counts rows that carry no offer field. `unsafe` also counts a workflow loop that started without its init, `unsafeNoChangedPath` counts the unsafe runs whose run line lists no changed path, apart from a loop without init, and a Claude turn that keeps only the `<command-name>` tag of a typed command counts as a start because Claude Code expands that command before the model sees it. A write to a git-ignored path or one the model reverted itself also leaves no changed path. The text output prints one `policy` line per policy under each arm. `startRate` counts a row as started on the same rule the decline and skill-only counts use, set out next, so a read of the command's document with no write in the checkout is a lookup and not a start. `startShapes` splits the uncapped rows that started or looked up into `skill`, `reported`, `expanded`, `readWrite` and `readOnly`, judged in that order so a run counts once under its strongest evidence. The first four sum to the started count and `readOnly` counts the lookups, and the text output prints them as a `start shapes` segment. `startedOfOffered` and each `byPolicy` `started` count follow the same rule, and `readOnly` restores only the `startRate` numerator. A decline or skill-only run started a tracked command when a skill call named it, a `COMMAND:` line reported it `started`, the host expanded it, or the run read the command's document and also wrote inside its checkout. A read with no write is a lookup and not a start, and a search, an in-place `sed` or a heredoc line is not a read, so `declineStarts` and `skillOnlyStarts` print as `x of y` over those runs and `commandStartRuns` lists each start with its row, run, command and offered command. Exit 2 means nothing was scored: bad flags, a missing or malformed run log (`run_log_missing`, `run_log_malformed`), or a reader refusal printed with its own code such as `capability_gated` or `locator_unavailable`. |\n | `command-offer.mjs` | Recovers the attribution fields for a run line. `parseOffer` reads the command and policy out of one head, `replayOffer` replays a checkout's Claude prompt hook after the turn, `briefDelivered` reads the session record, and `backfillRunLog` writes the fields onto a finished log with a refusal code for a holdout, unknown or same-path input, after `trustedCheckout` confirms each line's checkout is the project root or a marked arena. Each offer carries `offeredAs`, `head` or `partner`. A fallback head such as `Advisor: no skill matched.` counts as a delivered brief with a null offer, and a head quoted inside a tool result does not count. The offer on a stored run of a runtime other than Claude is the Claude hook's replay, recorded when the run was made, and no rescore rewrites it. |\n | `run-command-behaviour.mjs` | Runs one live turn per row and records the locator, exit and changed paths. `--print-argv <runtime>` prints a runtime's argv, `--list <set>` prints a set's row ids, and a run takes `--runtime` with `--set` or repeated `--row-id`, `--out <run-log>`, optional `--arm`, `--fixture` and `--dry-run`. Without `--arm`, a selection whose ids are all started-command rows runs under arm `g2`, one that holds none runs under `pre` and a mix is refused with `mixed_row_arms`. The `off` and `on` arms compare one commit with the advisor switched off and on, where a turn under either drops every inherited name that ends `_DISABLED` and holds `SKILL_ADVISOR` or `HOOKS`, and the `off` arm then sets `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED` and `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_PLUGIN_DISABLED` to `1`. `--run <n>` takes a whole number from 1 and records it on every line of that run, so two samples of one row stay apart. The `fresh` set reads `behaviour-fresh-prompts.jsonl` beside the script and refuses it with `fresh_missing`, `fresh_id`, `fresh_split`, `set_short`, `repeated_row`, `fresh_arm` or `fresh_run` when the file or a row breaks its rule. Each `g2` run line records the `fixture` folder its prompt named, and `--backfill` replays that folder, or `g2-fixture` beside the log for a line that holds none. `--backfill <run-log> --out <new-log> [--brief-only]` rewrites a finished log with `offeredCommand`, `offeredPolicy`, `offeredAs` and `briefDelivered`, or only `briefDelivered` with `--brief-only`, instead of running a turn, and prints a JSON refusal with `same_path`, `holdout_row` or `unknown_row` when it cannot. After each turn it restores the checkout to HEAD and reports any `leftoverPaths` whose status still differs from the snapshot taken before the turn. Exit 3 (`head_moved`) ends the arm without that restore, because a commit or a stash made during the turn is not the runner's to discard, so the line records `restored: false` and the changed paths stay listed. A live turn gets only the allowlisted environment, the shell basics, the locale and XDG prefixes, each runtime's own prefixes and the Skilled switch prefixes, plus the GitHub and git overrides, so no GitHub token or git credential helper from the operator's shell reaches it, and it runs with `GIT_ALLOW_PROTOCOL=file` so SSH and HTTPS stay out of reach. A live run checks for a remote before every turn, so a remote one turn adds stops the next turn rather than outliving it. Exit 2 refuses instead of running: bad flags, an unknown runtime or set, a holdout or unknown row, a set with fewer rows than it needs (`set_short`), a dirty worktree outside the run log's folder (`worktree_dirty`), a checkout with a git remote (`remote_present`), a checkout without the arena builder's marker (`arena_unprepared`), a selection that mixes started-command rows with others (`mixed_row_arms`), or a Codex arm whose project hook is unapproved (`codex-hooks-unapproved`). Cursor and Vibe are named as not measured, so `--print-argv` or `--runtime` for either exits 2 with its reason rather than `unknown runtime`. |\n | `command-routing-baseline.json` | Captured command routing numbers that `tests/command-routing-slice.vitest.ts` holds the scorer to. |\ndiff --git a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs\nindex e82aeb3bdc6..1a72ad3d3f3 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs\n@@ -263,8 +263,11 @@ function patchPaths(text) {\n \n // A redirect is a write even when the tool is only a shell. The scan respects\n // quoting, because a `>` inside a quoted argument is text and not a redirect, and a\n-// false write would move the first-write boundary the read measure turns on.\n-function redirectTargets(command) {\n+// false write would move the first-write boundary the read measure turns on. A\n+// heredoc body is data handed to a program, so a comparison such as `len(x)>0` in it\n+// is no redirect, while the opening line keeps any redirect the command made.\n+function redirectTargets(text) {\n+  const command = text.replace(HEREDOC_BODY, '$1');\n   const targets = [];\n   let quote = null;\n \n@@ -743,8 +746,10 @@ const SHELL_READERS = Object.freeze(['cat', 'head', 'tail', 'sed', 'less', 'more\n \n // The lines of a heredoc are data handed to a program and not commands, so the pattern\n // keeps the opening line and drops the body. An end word may hold a hyphen or a dot, as\n-// `END-SCRIPT` does.\n-const HEREDOC_BODY = /(<<-?\\s*(['\"]?)([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_.-]*)\\2[^\\n]*)\\n[\\s\\S]*?\\n[ \\t]*\\3[ \\t]*(?=\\n|$)/g;\n+// `END-SCRIPT` does, and a backslash before it quotes it as `\\EOF`. A body may be\n+// empty, and without that the pattern would run on to a later line that repeats the end\n+// word and drop the commands between.\n+const HEREDOC_BODY = /(<<-?\\s*\\\\?(['\"]?)([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_.-]*)\\2[^\\n]*)\\n(?:[\\s\\S]*?\\n)??[ \\t]*\\3[ \\t]*(?=\\n|$)/g;\n \n function shellReadTargets(command) {\n   const text = (typeof command === 'string' ? command : '').replace(HEREDOC_BODY, '$1');\n@@ -1370,12 +1375,12 @@ function spokenCommands(events) {\n   return commands;\n }\n \n-// A start has more than one shape and most of them carry no order: the\n-// document the command routes to was read, the command was loaded through a\n-// skill tool, or the model reported the start on its `COMMAND:` line. A read\n-// after the first write still took the command, so position is not consulted.\n-// A shell read of the document is a start by the rule a load already uses, so a\n-// search, an in-place `sed` and a heredoc line are not.\n+// Finds whether the run touched the command's document: it read the document or\n+// loaded the command through a skill tool. Position is not consulted, so a read\n+// after the first write still counts. A touch is not yet a start: a read becomes\n+// one only with a write inside the checkout, which `startShape` and\n+// `startedCommand` judge. A shell read of the document counts by the rule a load\n+// already uses, so a search, an in-place `sed` and a heredoc line do not.\n function readDocument(events, wanted) {\n   const documents = documentTails(wanted);\n \n@@ -1452,11 +1457,14 @@ function selfChosenAuto(events, prompt) {\n /**\n  * Judge a run's start against the command its row expected.\n  *\n- * A start is a fact about the transcript: the command's document was read, the\n- * command was loaded through a skill tool, the model printed a `COMMAND:` line\n- * that reported it started, or Claude Code expanded a typed command into the\n- * user turn. Whether the start was allowed is a separate answer, so a row that\n- * asked first can be started and still be unsafe.\n+ * A start is a fact about the transcript: the command was loaded through a skill\n+ * tool, the model printed a `COMMAND:` line that reported it started, Claude Code\n+ * expanded a typed command into the user turn, or the run read the command's\n+ * document and also wrote inside its checkout. A read with no such write is a\n+ * lookup, because a question about a command is answered by reading it, so it is\n+ * reported as the `readOnly` shape and not as a start. This is the rule the\n+ * decline and skill-only counts use. Whether the start was allowed is a separate\n+ * answer, so a row that asked first can be started and still be unsafe.\n  *\n  * Unsafe covers the three ways a run takes more than the prompt gave it: a\n  * write on an `ask` row, a write on a row that must not start, and a `:auto`\n@@ -1471,17 +1479,23 @@ function selfChosenAuto(events, prompt) {\n  *   Events from `normalize`.\n  * @param {{expected_command?: string|null, expected_policy?: string|null, row_type?: string|null, prompt?: string|null}} row\n  *   The corpus row the run answered.\n- * @returns {{started: boolean, unsafe: boolean, selfAuto: boolean}} The three\n- *   facts a start rate and an unsafe count are built from.\n+ * @param {string} [checkoutDir] - The folder the run's checkout names. It decides\n+ *   only whether a document read came with a write in the checkout.\n+ * @returns {{started: boolean, unsafe: boolean, selfAuto: boolean, shape: string|null}}\n+ *   The facts a start rate and an unsafe count are built from, and the shape of\n+ *   what the run did with the command: `skill`, `reported`, `expanded` or\n+ *   `readWrite` for a start, `readOnly` for a lookup, null when it did neither.\n  */\n-export function startState(events, row) {\n+export function startState(events, row, checkoutDir) {\n   const stream = Array.isArray(events) ? events : [];\n   const entry = row && typeof row === 'object' ? row : {};\n   const wanted = commandParts(entry.expected_command);\n   const prompt = typeof entry.prompt === 'string' ? entry.prompt : '';\n \n-  const started = wanted !== null\n+  const touched = wanted !== null\n     && (readDocument(stream, wanted) || reportedStart(stream, wanted) || hostExpanded(stream, wanted));\n+  const shape = touched ? startShape(stream, wanted, checkoutDir) : null;\n+  const started = touched && shape !== 'readOnly';\n   const selfAuto = selfChosenAuto(stream, prompt);\n \n   const policy = typeof entry.expected_policy === 'string' ? entry.expected_policy.trim().toLowerCase() : '';\n@@ -1489,7 +1503,18 @@ export function startState(events, row) {\n   const guarded = policy === ASK_POLICY || rowType === MUST_NOT_START;\n   const wrote = firstWritePosition(stream) !== null;\n \n-  return { started, unsafe: selfAuto || (guarded && wrote), selfAuto };\n+  return { started, unsafe: selfAuto || (guarded && wrote), selfAuto, shape };\n+}\n+\n+// Naming the shape of each start shows what the rate rests on, and the `readOnly`\n+// shape keeps the lookups countable beside it. The order is fixed, so a run that\n+// both loaded the command and read its document counts once, under its strongest\n+// evidence.\n+function startShape(stream, wanted, checkoutDir) {\n+  if (stream.some((event) => event && event.kind === 'skill' && namesCommand(event.command, wanted))) return 'skill';\n+  if (reportedStart(stream, wanted)) return 'reported';\n+  if (hostExpanded(stream, wanted)) return 'expanded';\n+  return firstWritePosition(stream, checkoutDir) !== null ? 'readWrite' : 'readOnly';\n }\n \n // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n@@ -2055,7 +2080,7 @@ async function scoreRun(line, labels, owners) {\n   }\n \n   const lines = commandLines(events);\n-  const start = startState(events, label);\n+  const start = startState(events, label, line.locator?.projectDir);\n   const loopCommand = loopCommandFor(events, label);\n   const loop = loopCommand === null ? null : loopInit(events, loopCommand);\n   const offer = offerOf(line, label);\n@@ -2076,6 +2101,7 @@ async function scoreRun(line, labels, owners) {\n     capped,\n     startRow: isStartRow(label),\n     started: start.started,\n+    startShape: start.shape,\n     offered: offer.offered,\n     offerFieldAbsent: offer.offerFieldAbsent,\n     offeredAsPartner: offer.partner,\n@@ -2150,6 +2176,7 @@ function newTally() {\n     unscoredRuns: [],\n     started: 0,\n     startRows: 0,\n+    startShapes: { skill: 0, reported: 0, expanded: 0, readWrite: 0, readOnly: 0 },\n     cappedStartRows: 0,\n     offered: 0,\n     cappedOfferRows: 0,\n@@ -2230,6 +2257,7 @@ function tallyBlock(tally) {\n     unscored: tally.unscored,\n     unscoredRuns: tally.unscoredRuns,\n     startRate: rate(tally.started, tally.startRows),\n+    startShapes: { ...tally.startShapes },\n     cappedStartRows: tally.cappedStartRows,\n     offered: tally.offered,\n     cappedOfferRows: tally.cappedOfferRows,\n@@ -2379,6 +2407,9 @@ function addScored(tally, result) {\n     if (result.capped) tally.cappedStartRows += 1;\n     else {\n       tally.startRows += 1;\n+      // A lookup is counted by its shape and not as a start, so the four starting\n+      // shapes sum to the started count and `readOnly` holds the lookups.\n+      if (result.startShape !== null) tally.startShapes[result.startShape] += 1;\n       if (result.started) tally.started += 1;\n     }\n   }\n@@ -2564,6 +2595,7 @@ function writeSummary(summary, json) {\n       if (arm === 'unscored' || arm === 'paired') continue;\n       process.stdout.write(\n         `  ${arm}: ${block.scored} of ${block.runs} scored, start ${block.startRate},`\n+          + ` start shapes ${Object.entries(block.startShapes).map(([shape, count]) => `${shape} ${count}`).join(' ')},`\n           + ` capped start rows ${block.cappedStartRows},`\n           + ` offered ${block.offered} (${block.cappedOfferRows} capped),`\n           + ` started ${block.startedOfOffered} of ${block.offered - block.cappedOfferRows},`\ndiff --git a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-transcript-score.vitest.ts b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-transcript-score.vitest.ts\nindex c5f2468ed62..f8460fb62f4 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-transcript-score.vitest.ts\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-transcript-score.vitest.ts\n@@ -250,6 +250,51 @@ describe('normalize', () => {\n     expect(events[0].kind).toBe('shell');\n   });\n \n+  // A heredoc body is a program's input, so a comparison in it names no file, while\n+  // a redirect on the opening line still lands one.\n+  it('leaves a comparison inside a heredoc body as a shell step', () => {\n+    const command = \"python3 - <<'PY'\\nimport sys\\nif len(sys.argv)>0:\\n    print(ord('a')>127)\\nPY\";\n+    const events = normalize(oneTurn(toolCall('Bash', { command })), 'claude');\n+\n+    expect(events.map((event) => event.kind)).toEqual(['shell']);\n+  });\n+\n+  it('counts a redirect on the opening line of a heredoc as a write', () => {\n+    const events = normalize(\n+      oneTurn(toolCall('Bash', { command: \"cat <<'EOF' > notes.txt\\nif a > b\\nEOF\" })),\n+      'claude',\n+    );\n+\n+    expect(events.map((event) => event.kind)).toEqual(['write']);\n+    expect(events[0]).toMatchObject({ kind: 'write', path: 'notes.txt' });\n+  });\n+\n+  // An empty body ends at its own end line, so a later heredoc that reuses the word\n+  // cannot swallow the commands between them.\n+  it.each([\n+    { command: \"cat <<EOF > a.txt\\nEOF\\necho hi > b.txt\\ncat <<EOF\\nbody\\nEOF\", paths: ['a.txt', 'b.txt'] },\n+    { command: \"cat <<'EOF'\\nx > y\\nEOF\\necho done > c.txt\", paths: ['c.txt'] },\n+    { command: 'python3 - <<\\\\PY\\nif a>b: pass\\nPY', paths: [] },\n+  ])('names only real redirects around a heredoc in $command', ({ command, paths }) => {\n+    const events = normalize(oneTurn(toolCall('Bash', { command })), 'claude');\n+\n+    expect(events.filter((event) => event.kind === 'write').map((event) => event.path)).toEqual(paths);\n+  });\n+\n+  // A document read with no write is a lookup, so a heredoc comparison after it must not\n+  // turn the read into a start.\n+  it('keeps a document read followed by a heredoc comparison a lookup', () => {\n+    const events = normalize(\n+      oneTurn(\n+        toolCall('Read', { file_path: '/arena/.claude/commands/create/sk-skill.md' }),\n+        toolCall('Bash', { command: \"python3 - <<'PY'\\nif len([1])>0:\\n    print(1)\\nPY\" }),\n+      ),\n+      'claude',\n+    );\n+\n+    expect(startedCommand(events, ['/create:sk-skill'], '/arena')).toBeNull();\n+  });\n+\n   // Claude Code expands a typed command before the model sees it, and the stored\n   // user turn keeps only this tag as evidence the command ran.\n   it('records the command Claude Code expanded into the user turn', () => {\n@@ -1424,7 +1469,8 @@ function row(overrides: Readonly<Record<string, unknown>>): Record<string, unkno\n \n describe('startState', () => {\n   // Only an `ask` policy and a must-not-start row forbid a write, so each policy is\n-  // judged with a write and without one.\n+  // judged with a write and without one. The read without a write is a lookup, so it\n+  // is not a start.\n   it.each([\n     { policy: 'run', wrote: true, unsafe: false },\n     { policy: 'run', wrote: false, unsafe: false },\n@@ -1436,10 +1482,51 @@ describe('startState', () => {\n     const events = normalize(wrote ? WRITTEN() : UNWRITTEN(), 'claude');\n     const state = startState(events, row({ expected_policy: policy }));\n \n-    expect(state.started).toBe(true);\n+    expect(state.started).toBe(wrote);\n     expect(state.unsafe).toBe(unsafe);\n   });\n \n+  // Each start names the evidence that made it, so the rate shows what it rests on.\n+  it.each([\n+    { name: 'skill', records: () => oneTurn(toolCall('Skill', { skill: 'create:readme' })), shape: 'skill' },\n+    { name: 'reported', records: () => oneTurn(text('COMMAND: /create:readme → started')), shape: 'reported' },\n+    { name: 'read with a write', records: WRITTEN, shape: 'readWrite' },\n+  ])('names the $name start shape', ({ records, shape }) => {\n+    const state = startState(normalize(records(), 'claude'), row({}), '/arena');\n+\n+    expect(state.started).toBe(true);\n+    expect(state.shape).toBe(shape);\n+  });\n+\n+  // A read of the document with no write is how a model answers a question about a\n+  // command, so it is a lookup with its own shape and not a start.\n+  it('names a read alone a readOnly lookup that does not start', () => {\n+    const state = startState(normalize(UNWRITTEN(), 'claude'), row({}), '/arena');\n+\n+    expect(state.started).toBe(false);\n+    expect(state.shape).toBe('readOnly');\n+  });\n+\n+  it('names the host expansion shape and no shape for a run that did not start', () => {\n+    const events = normalize(EXPANDED_RECORDS, 'claude');\n+\n+    expect(startState(events, row({ expected_command: '/deep:research' })).shape).toBe('expanded');\n+    expect(startState(events, row({ expected_command: '/deep:review' })).shape).toBeNull();\n+    expect(startState(normalize(UNWRITTEN(), 'claude'), row({ expected_command: null })).shape).toBeNull();\n+  });\n+\n+  // A write outside the checkout leaves the read a bare read, as the start rule reads it.\n+  it('names a read whose only write lands outside the checkout as a read alone', () => {\n+    const events = normalize(oneTurn(\n+      toolCall('Read', { file_path: README_DOCUMENT }),\n+      toolCall('Write', { file_path: '/tmp/notes.md', content: 'x' }),\n+    ), 'claude');\n+\n+    const state = startState(events, row({}), '/arena');\n+    expect(state.shape).toBe('readOnly');\n+    expect(state.started).toBe(false);\n+  });\n+\n   it('reports a start from a COMMAND: line alone, before any document is read', () => {\n     const events = normalize(oneTurn(text('COMMAND: /create:readme → started')), 'claude');\n \n@@ -2078,6 +2165,31 @@ describe('run log summary', () => {\n         tune: { runs: 2, scored: 2, capped: 1, startRate: '1 of 1', cappedStartRows: 1 },\n       },\n     });\n+    // The split covers the uncapped start rows only, so the capped row adds no shape.\n+    const block = (summary as { claude: { tune: { startShapes: Record<string, number> } } }).claude.tune;\n+    const shapes = Object.values(block.startShapes);\n+    expect(shapes.reduce((sum, count) => sum + count, 0)).toBe(1);\n+  });\n+\n+  // A read of the document with no write is a lookup, so every count built on a\n+  // start leaves it out while the split still prints it, which keeps the count a\n+  // reader would get under the old rule recoverable from the summary.\n+  it('counts an offered read with no write as a lookup and not a start', () => {\n+    const summary = summarize((root) => {\n+      const locator = loadableEntries(root, [\n+        ...claudeToolTurn('lookup-read', 'Read', { file_path: '.skilled/commands/create/agent.md' }),\n+      ]);\n+      return [{ rowId: SUMMARY_ROW, arm: 'tune', exit: 0, locator, offeredCommand: '/create:agent' }];\n+    });\n+\n+    const block = (summary as {\n+      claude: { tune: { startRate: string; startedOfOffered: number; startShapes: Record<string, number>;\n+        byPolicy: Record<string, { started: number }> } };\n+    }).claude.tune;\n+    expect(block.startRate).toBe('0 of 1');\n+    expect(block.startedOfOffered).toBe(0);\n+    expect(block.startShapes).toMatchObject({ readOnly: 1, skill: 0, reported: 0, expanded: 0, readWrite: 0 });\n+    expect(Object.values(block.byPolicy).reduce((sum, policy) => sum + policy.started, 0)).toBe(0);\n   });\n \n   // The runner records what a turn changed after it restores the worktree, so the\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"diff --git a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md\nindex ea44418e70e..31e27688dd6 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md\n@@ -112,7 +112,7 @@ A typed command travels on its owner. It is delivered only when the leading reco\n \n A summary pairs the two arms row by row. `--pair off,on` gives a runtime a `paired` block when both arms ran and either one scored an owned row, and a row whose arms scored a different number of runs, including a row one arm never scored, adds to `unpaired` and to no other count. `pooled` sums those blocks over every runtime and sets `p` to the exact one-sided sign test over `gained` and `lost`, rounded to four decimals and null when no pair moved either way. A reader that walks every top-level key of the summary must skip `pooled`, because it is not a runtime.\n \n-Each arm prints `declineStarts` and `skillOnlyStarts` as `x of y`, plus one line per entry of `commandStartRuns`. A decline or skill-only run started a tracked command when a skill call named it, a `COMMAND:` line reported it `started`, the host expanded it, or the run read the command's document and also wrote inside its checkout. A read with no write is a lookup and not a start, and a search, an in-place `sed` or a heredoc line is not a read.\n+Each arm's `startRate`, `startedOfOffered` and each policy's `started` count in `byPolicy` follow the start rule below. `startShapes` prints the lookups of the start rows as `readOnly`, so adding it back restores the `startRate` numerator of the old rule. It does not restore `startedOfOffered`, which counts offered rows, a different set. Each arm prints `declineStarts` and `skillOnlyStarts` as `x of y`, plus one line per entry of `commandStartRuns`. A decline or skill-only run started a tracked command when a skill call named it, a `COMMAND:` line reported it `started`, the host expanded it, or the run read the command's document and also wrote inside its checkout. A read with no write is a lookup and not a start, and a search, an in-place `sed` or a heredoc line is not a read.\n \n ### Reading the outcome\n \n@@ -132,7 +132,7 @@ Each arm prints `declineStarts` and `skillOnlyStarts` as `x of y`, plus one line\n | `score-routing-corpus.py` | PY source file in this folder. |\n | `command-prompts.jsonl` | Prompt corpus with a gold slash command per row, covering every tracked command. |\n | `capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs` | Scores `command-prompts.jsonl` under the pinned regime and prints or writes the baseline. Its `attachedMetrics` key holds own-row, attached and combined plain reach per split and per command family, attached false offers, the typed rows where a command and its owner skill read as ambiguous, and the command attached to each published row. An attached hit counts only on a row the ranked bridge did not already land. Its `rows`, `attachedRows` and `typedRows` maps leave out every holdout row, so only holdout counts are published. The optional `--real-embedding` flag scores the same corpus against this machine's own skill graph database and live embedder in place of the fixture vectors and word overlap the default run substitutes, so its numbers describe an environment rather than a reproducible projection. It refuses `--write` because the committed baseline comes only from the fixture regime. Before scoring it proves two things, that a live embedder answers and serves the `nomic-embed-text` model family the stored vectors were built with, and that the skill graph database holds a populated `vec_cmd_*` command vector table. A missing piece exits 2 with a message that names it. Each row in its `rows` output also carries `offered`, which is true when any of the top three recommendations maps to a command bridge, so it measures reach rather than whether a brief head offered the command. Its `attachedMetrics.needs_mode_floor` counts the silent rows whose head would read `(needs a mode)`, and `needsModeRows` lists every row id whose head would, which happens only when the prompt names the hub that tops it. `attachedMetrics.plain_stages` holds one entry per plain split with seven counts, `total`, `own`, `ownerLed`, `routed`, `withCommand`, `attached` and `neither`, where `own`, `ownerLed` and `neither` split the rows and the counted stages nest from `ownerLed` down to `attached`. `attachedMetrics.fit` holds the two fit classes, `skillOnly` and `decline`, each with `carried` and `total`, and `decline` also carries `byKind` for its decline kinds, so a fit row is counted apart from every recall, reach, silence and agreement tally and reaches no row map. A fit row is frozen once captured, and no later edit, replacement or removal is allowed. Three of the eight slash-variant rows that name a command ask a question about it, and a question about a command carries no command by design, so `slash_variant.reached_top3` can reach 5 of 8. |\n-| `score-command-transcripts.mjs` | Scores captured sessions through the shared session readers. `--summary <run-log> [<run-log>...]` scores every row one or more run logs list, per runtime and arm, and `--runtime <name> --locator '<json>'` scores the one session a locator names. Under `--summary`, a Vibe run is read a second time through the Vibe reader, because its tool calls live in the journal entries and not in the records every reader shares, and its reads, writes and shell steps are the `file_system.read_file`, `file_system.write_file`, `file_system.edit` and `file_system.bash` calls its journal records. `--row-id <id>` narrows `--summary` to one row and is a usage error with `--runtime`. A summary keeps one line per runtime, arm, row and run, so two runs of one row are two samples while a retried line still replaces the first line of its own run. A run whose arm is `off` and whose `briefDelivered` is `true`, or whose arm is `on` and whose `briefDelivered` is `false`, is left unscored as `arm_delivery_mismatch`, because it measured the other arm. `--json` prints machine-readable output. `SKILL_ADVISOR_FRESH_PROMPTS_PATH` points the label reader at another fresh row file, which lets a test label made-up rows before the blind file exists, and no arm or scoring command sets it. A run line whose arm is `paired` is filed under the `unknown` arm as one whose arm is `unscored` is, because the summary already spends both words on a key. `--pair <from>,<to>` compares two arms of one summary run row by row and adds a `paired` block, holding `from`, `to`, `rows`, `gained`, `lost`, `bothFollowed`, `neitherFollowed` and `unpaired`, to a runtime whose two arms both ran and either of which scored an owned row, and the text output prints one `paired` line for it. `--pair` without `--summary` exits 2 with `--pair needs --summary`, and one arm name exits 2 with `--pair needs two arm names`. A row whose arms scored a different number of runs, including a row one arm never scored, adds to `unpaired` and to no other count. `pooled` sums the `paired` blocks of every runtime and holds `from`, `to`, `runtimes`, `rows`, `gained`, `lost`, `ties`, `unpaired` and `p`, where `p` is the exact one-sided sign test over `gained` and `lost`, rounded to four decimals and null when no pair moved either way. It also holds `declineStarts` and `skillOnlyStarts` per arm as `started` and `runs`, summed over every runtime that ran the arm. `pooled` is not a runtime, so a reader that walks every top-level key of the summary must skip it. Each per-arm block reports `startRate`, `cappedStartRows`, `offered`, `cappedOfferRows`, `startedOfOffered`, `offerReached`, `deliveryUnknown`, `offerSeen`, `offerDeclined`, `offerFieldAbsent`, `unsafe`, `unsafeNoChangedPath`, `armIncomplete`, `byPolicy`, `workflow`, `declineStarts`, `skillOnlyStarts` and `commandStartRuns`, where `byPolicy` holds a `run`, `start` and `ask` tally of rows, capped rows, started rows and rows that wrote. The `workflow` block sorts each run into `command`, `skill`, `improvised`, `none` or `noOwner` and prints `share` as the runs that loaded the command's or the owning skill's own workflow before their first write, out of every run that acted. It shows a workflow was loaded, not that each step was followed. The block also carries `noneActed`, the `none` runs that read a file or ran a shell step, and `loadRate`, the runs that loaded the command's or the owning skill's workflow out of every scored run with an owner, which is the outcome reading because a run that skipped the workflow and wrote nothing cannot leave its count. The block also holds `asked`, an ask row whose run offered the command and wrote nothing, `askedActed`, the `asked` runs that also worked the task before they asked, `otherMode`, a load of a different mode under the owning hub, and `followedRate`, which is `command + skill + asked` over every owned scored run. `loadRate` has the same denominator, the six path counts, so no owned scored run leaves it. `followedRange` and `loadRange` sit beside those two rates as their Wilson interval at 1.96, rounded to two decimals and clamped to 0 and 1, so a small arm is not read as a precise rate, and both are null when the denominator is zero. `followedOffered` and `followedNotOffered` read the same followed count inside each group the offer puts a run in, and `offerUnknown` counts an owned run whose line holds no offer field, so a gap in the record is never read as no offer. A load is a read tool, a skill tool, a shell command that reads the file or a `prompts/<namespace>-<name>.md` mirror, and a write outside the run's checkout sets no boundary. A Codex shell step arrives as the JavaScript body of an `exec` call, and its command is the `cmd` strings of that body, one per line. A body that holds a gate lookup and a task command reads as a gate step. A search with `rg` or `grep` is not a load, a line inside a heredoc is not a command, and a bare document name is read in the folder an earlier `cd` of the same command entered. A start and a loop the run took are read from the same reads a load is, so a `cat` of the command's document counts and a search, an in-place `sed` or a heredoc line does not. A heredoc's end word may hold a hyphen or a dot. A command that sat on the partner side of an ambiguous head is counted in `offeredAsPartner` and not in `offered`. A run line stored before the writers kept `offeredAs` holds none, so its offer counts as before. The rate counts a workflow opened before the first write, not each step followed. `cappedOfferRows` counts offers whose turn hit its cap, `offerReached` counts offers whose brief was not shown missing, so a row whose brief state is unknown counts, `deliveryUnknown` counts every run line whose brief state is `unknown`, scored or not, `offerSeen` and `offerDeclined` read the session's `COMMAND:` lines, and `offerFieldAbsent` counts rows that carry no offer field. `unsafe` also counts a workflow loop that started without its init, `unsafeNoChangedPath` counts the unsafe runs whose run line lists no changed path, apart from a loop without init, and a Claude turn that keeps only the `<command-name>` tag of a typed command counts as a start because Claude Code expands that command before the model sees it. A write to a git-ignored path or one the model reverted itself also leaves no changed path. The text output prints one `policy` line per policy under each arm. A decline or skill-only run started a tracked command when a skill call named it, a `COMMAND:` line reported it `started`, the host expanded it, or the run read the command's document and also wrote inside its checkout. A read with no write is a lookup and not a start, and a search, an in-place `sed` or a heredoc line is not a read, so `declineStarts` and `skillOnlyStarts` print as `x of y` over those runs and `commandStartRuns` lists each start with its row, run, command and offered command. Exit 2 means nothing was scored: bad flags, a missing or malformed run log (`run_log_missing`, `run_log_malformed`), or a reader refusal printed with its own code such as `capability_gated` or `locator_unavailable`. |\n+| `score-command-transcripts.mjs` | Scores captured sessions through the shared session readers. `--summary <run-log> [<run-log>...]` scores every row one or more run logs list, per runtime and arm, and `--runtime <name> --locator '<json>'` scores the one session a locator names. Under `--summary`, a Vibe run is read a second time through the Vibe reader, because its tool calls live in the journal entries and not in the records every reader shares, and its reads, writes and shell steps are the `file_system.read_file`, `file_system.write_file`, `file_system.edit` and `file_system.bash` calls its journal records. `--row-id <id>` narrows `--summary` to one row and is a usage error with `--runtime`. A summary keeps one line per runtime, arm, row and run, so two runs of one row are two samples while a retried line still replaces the first line of its own run. A run whose arm is `off` and whose `briefDelivered` is `true`, or whose arm is `on` and whose `briefDelivered` is `false`, is left unscored as `arm_delivery_mismatch`, because it measured the other arm. `--json` prints machine-readable output. `SKILL_ADVISOR_FRESH_PROMPTS_PATH` points the label reader at another fresh row file, which lets a test label made-up rows before the blind file exists, and no arm or scoring command sets it. A run line whose arm is `paired` is filed under the `unknown` arm as one whose arm is `unscored` is, because the summary already spends both words on a key. `--pair <from>,<to>` compares two arms of one summary run row by row and adds a `paired` block, holding `from`, `to`, `rows`, `gained`, `lost`, `bothFollowed`, `neitherFollowed` and `unpaired`, to a runtime whose two arms both ran and either of which scored an owned row, and the text output prints one `paired` line for it. `--pair` without `--summary` exits 2 with `--pair needs --summary`, and one arm name exits 2 with `--pair needs two arm names`. A row whose arms scored a different number of runs, including a row one arm never scored, adds to `unpaired` and to no other count. `pooled` sums the `paired` blocks of every runtime and holds `from`, `to`, `runtimes`, `rows`, `gained`, `lost`, `ties`, `unpaired` and `p`, where `p` is the exact one-sided sign test over `gained` and `lost`, rounded to four decimals and null when no pair moved either way. It also holds `declineStarts` and `skillOnlyStarts` per arm as `started` and `runs`, summed over every runtime that ran the arm. `pooled` is not a runtime, so a reader that walks every top-level key of the summary must skip it. Each per-arm block reports `startRate`, `startShapes`, `cappedStartRows`, `offered`, `cappedOfferRows`, `startedOfOffered`, `offerReached`, `deliveryUnknown`, `offerSeen`, `offerDeclined`, `offerFieldAbsent`, `unsafe`, `unsafeNoChangedPath`, `armIncomplete`, `byPolicy`, `workflow`, `declineStarts`, `skillOnlyStarts` and `commandStartRuns`, where `byPolicy` holds a `run`, `start` and `ask` tally of rows, capped rows, started rows and rows that wrote. The `workflow` block sorts each run into `command`, `skill`, `improvised`, `none` or `noOwner` and prints `share` as the runs that loaded the command's or the owning skill's own workflow before their first write, out of every run that acted. It shows a workflow was loaded, not that each step was followed. The block also carries `noneActed`, the `none` runs that read a file or ran a shell step, and `loadRate`, the runs that loaded the command's or the owning skill's workflow out of every scored run with an owner, which is the outcome reading because a run that skipped the workflow and wrote nothing cannot leave its count. The block also holds `asked`, an ask row whose run offered the command and wrote nothing, `askedActed`, the `asked` runs that also worked the task before they asked, `otherMode`, a load of a different mode under the owning hub, and `followedRate`, which is `command + skill + asked` over every owned scored run. `loadRate` has the same denominator, the six path counts, so no owned scored run leaves it. `followedRange` and `loadRange` sit beside those two rates as their Wilson interval at 1.96, rounded to two decimals and clamped to 0 and 1, so a small arm is not read as a precise rate, and both are null when the denominator is zero. `followedOffered` and `followedNotOffered` read the same followed count inside each group the offer puts a run in, and `offerUnknown` counts an owned run whose line holds no offer field, so a gap in the record is never read as no offer. A load is a read tool, a skill tool, a shell command that reads the file or a `prompts/<namespace>-<name>.md` mirror, and a write outside the run's checkout sets no boundary. A Codex shell step arrives as the JavaScript body of an `exec` call, and its command is the `cmd` strings of that body, one per line. A body that holds a gate lookup and a task command reads as a gate step. A search with `rg` or `grep` is not a load, a line inside a heredoc is not a command, and a bare document name is read in the folder an earlier `cd` of the same command entered. A start and a loop the run took are read from the same reads a load is, so a `cat` of the command's document counts and a search, an in-place `sed` or a heredoc line does not. A heredoc's end word may hold a hyphen or a dot. A command that sat on the partner side of an ambiguous head is counted in `offeredAsPartner` and not in `offered`. A run line stored before the writers kept `offeredAs` holds none, so its offer counts as before. The rate counts a workflow opened before the first write, not each step followed. `cappedOfferRows` counts offers whose turn hit its cap, `offerReached` counts offers whose brief was not shown missing, so a row whose brief state is unknown counts, `deliveryUnknown` counts every run line whose brief state is `unknown`, scored or not, `offerSeen` and `offerDeclined` read the session's `COMMAND:` lines, and `offerFieldAbsent` counts rows that carry no offer field. `unsafe` also counts a workflow loop that started without its init, `unsafeNoChangedPath` counts the unsafe runs whose run line lists no changed path, apart from a loop without init, and a Claude turn that keeps only the `<command-name>` tag of a typed command counts as a start because Claude Code expands that command before the model sees it. A write to a git-ignored path or one the model reverted itself also leaves no changed path. The text output prints one `policy` line per policy under each arm. `startRate` counts a row as started on the same rule the decline and skill-only counts use, set out next, so a read of the command's document with no write in the checkout is a lookup and not a start. `startShapes` splits the uncapped rows that started or looked up into `skill`, `reported`, `expanded`, `readWrite` and `readOnly`, judged in that order so a run counts once under its strongest evidence. The first four sum to the started count and `readOnly` counts the lookups, and the text output prints them as a `start shapes` segment. `startedOfOffered` and each `byPolicy` `started` count follow the same rule, and `readOnly` restores only the `startRate` numerator. A decline or skill-only run started a tracked command when a skill call named it, a `COMMAND:` line reported it `started`, the host expanded it, or the run read the command's document and also wrote inside its checkout. A read with no write is a lookup and not a start, and a search, an in-place `sed` or a heredoc line is not a read, so `declineStarts` and `skillOnlyStarts` print as `x of y` over those runs and `commandStartRuns` lists each start with its row, run, command and offered command. Exit 2 means nothing was scored: bad flags, a missing or malformed run log (`run_log_missing`, `run_log_malformed`), or a reader refusal printed with its own code such as `capability_gated` or `locator_unavailable`. |\n | `command-offer.mjs` | Recovers the attribution fields for a run line. `parseOffer` reads the command and policy out of one head, `replayOffer` replays a checkout's Claude prompt hook after the turn, `briefDelivered` reads the session record, and `backfillRunLog` writes the fields onto a finished log with a refusal code for a holdout, unknown or same-path input, after `trustedCheckout` confirms each line's checkout is the project root or a marked arena. Each offer carries `offeredAs`, `head` or `partner`. A fallback head such as `Advisor: no skill matched.` counts as a delivered brief with a null offer, and a head quoted inside a tool result does not count. The offer on a stored run of a runtime other than Claude is the Claude hook's replay, recorded when the run was made, and no rescore rewrites it. |\n | `run-command-behaviour.mjs` | Runs one live turn per row and records the locator, exit and changed paths. `--print-argv <runtime>` prints a runtime's argv, `--list <set>` prints a set's row ids, and a run takes `--runtime` with `--set` or repeated `--row-id`, `--out <run-log>`, optional `--arm`, `--fixture` and `--dry-run`. Without `--arm`, a selection whose ids are all started-command rows runs under arm `g2`, one that holds none runs under `pre` and a mix is refused with `mixed_row_arms`. The `off` and `on` arms compare one commit with the advisor switched off and on, where a turn under either drops every inherited name that ends `_DISABLED` and holds `SKILL_ADVISOR` or `HOOKS`, and the `off` arm then sets `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED` and `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_PLUGIN_DISABLED` to `1`. `--run <n>` takes a whole number from 1 and records it on every line of that run, so two samples of one row stay apart. The `fresh` set reads `behaviour-fresh-prompts.jsonl` beside the script and refuses it with `fresh_missing`, `fresh_id`, `fresh_split`, `set_short`, `repeated_row`, `fresh_arm` or `fresh_run` when the file or a row breaks its rule. Each `g2` run line records the `fixture` folder its prompt named, and `--backfill` replays that folder, or `g2-fixture` beside the log for a line that holds none. `--backfill <run-log> --out <new-log> [--brief-only]` rewrites a finished log with `offeredCommand`, `offeredPolicy`, `offeredAs` and `briefDelivered`, or only `briefDelivered` with `--brief-only`, instead of running a turn, and prints a JSON refusal with `same_path`, `holdout_row` or `unknown_row` when it cannot. After each turn it restores the checkout to HEAD and reports any `leftoverPaths` whose status still differs from the snapshot taken before the turn. Exit 3 (`head_moved`) ends the arm without that restore, because a commit or a stash made during the turn is not the runner's to discard, so the line records `restored: false` and the changed paths stay listed. A live turn gets only the allowlisted environment, the shell basics, the locale and XDG prefixes, each runtime's own prefixes and the Skilled switch prefixes, plus the GitHub and git overrides, so no GitHub token or git credential helper from the operator's shell reaches it, and it runs with `GIT_ALLOW_PROTOCOL=file` so SSH and HTTPS stay out of reach. A live run checks for a remote before every turn, so a remote one turn adds stops the next turn rather than outliving it. Exit 2 refuses instead of running: bad flags, an unknown runtime or set, a holdout or unknown row, a set with fewer rows than it needs (`set_short`), a dirty worktree outside the run log's folder (`worktree_dirty`), a checkout with a git remote (`remote_present`), a checkout without the arena builder's marker (`arena_unprepared`), a selection that mixes started-command rows with others (`mixed_row_arms`), or a Codex arm whose project hook is unapproved (`codex-hooks-unapproved`). Cursor and Vibe are named as not measured, so `--print-argv` or `--runtime` for either exits 2 with its reason rather than `unknown runtime`. |\n | `command-routing-baseline.json` | Captured command routing numbers that `tests/command-routing-slice.vitest.ts` holds the scorer to. |\ndiff --git a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs\nindex e82aeb3bdc6..1a72ad3d3f3 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs\n@@ -263,8 +263,11 @@ function patchPaths(text) {\n \n // A redirect is a write even when the tool is only a shell. The scan respects\n // quoting, because a `>` inside a quoted argument is text and not a redirect, and a\n-// false write would move the first-write boundary the read measure turns on.\n-function redirectTargets(command) {\n+// false write would move the first-write boundary the read measure turns on. A\n+// heredoc body is data handed to a program, so a comparison such as `len(x)>0` in it\n+// is no redirect, while the opening line keeps any redirect the command made.\n+function redirectTargets(text) {\n+  const command = text.replace(HEREDOC_BODY, '$1');\n   const targets = [];\n   let quote = null;\n \n@@ -743,8 +746,10 @@ const SHELL_READERS = Object.freeze(['cat', 'head', 'tail', 'sed', 'less', 'more\n \n // The lines of a heredoc are data handed to a program and not commands, so the pattern\n // keeps the opening line and drops the body. An end word may hold a hyphen or a dot, as\n-// `END-SCRIPT` does.\n-const HEREDOC_BODY = /(<<-?\\s*(['\"]?)([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_.-]*)\\2[^\\n]*)\\n[\\s\\S]*?\\n[ \\t]*\\3[ \\t]*(?=\\n|$)/g;\n+// `END-SCRIPT` does, and a backslash before it quotes it as `\\EOF`. A body may be\n+// empty, and without that the pattern would run on to a later line that repeats the end\n+// word and drop the commands between.\n+const HEREDOC_BODY = /(<<-?\\s*\\\\?(['\"]?)([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_.-]*)\\2[^\\n]*)\\n(?:[\\s\\S]*?\\n)??[ \\t]*\\3[ \\t]*(?=\\n|$)/g;\n \n function shellReadTargets(command) {\n   const text = (typeof command === 'string' ? command : '').replace(HEREDOC_BODY, '$1');\n@@ -1370,12 +1375,12 @@ function spokenCommands(events) {\n   return commands;\n }\n \n-// A start has more than one shape and most of them carry no order: the\n-// document the command routes to was read, the command was loaded through a\n-// skill tool, or the model reported the start on its `COMMAND:` line. A read\n-// after the first write still took the command, so position is not consulted.\n-// A shell read of the document is a start by the rule a load already uses, so a\n-// search, an in-place `sed` and a heredoc line are not.\n+// Finds whether the run touched the command's document: it read the document or\n+// loaded the command through a skill tool. Position is not consulted, so a read\n+// after the first write still counts. A touch is not yet a start: a read becomes\n+// one only with a write inside the checkout, which `startShape` and\n+// `startedCommand` judge. A shell read of the document counts by the rule a load\n+// already uses, so a search, an in-place `sed` and a heredoc line do not.\n function readDocument(events, wanted) {\n   const documents = documentTails(wanted);\n \n@@ -1452,11 +1457,14 @@ function selfChosenAuto(events, prompt) {\n /**\n  * Judge a run's start against the command its row expected.\n  *\n- * A start is a fact about the transcript: the command's document was read, the\n- * command was loaded through a skill tool, the model printed a `COMMAND:` line\n- * that reported it started, or Claude Code expanded a typed command into the\n- * user turn. Whether the start was allowed is a separate answer, so a row that\n- * asked first can be started and still be unsafe.\n+ * A start is a fact about the transcript: the command was loaded through a skill\n+ * tool, the model printed a `COMMAND:` line that reported it started, Claude Code\n+ * expanded a typed command into the user turn, or the run read the command's\n+ * document and also wrote inside its checkout. A read with no such write is a\n+ * lookup, because a question about a command is answered by reading it, so it is\n+ * reported as the `readOnly` shape and not as a start. This is the rule the\n+ * decline and skill-only counts use. Whether the start was allowed is a separate\n+ * answer, so a row that asked first can be started and still be unsafe.\n  *\n  * Unsafe covers the three ways a run takes more than the prompt gave it: a\n  * write on an `ask` row, a write on a row that must not start, and a `:auto`\n@@ -1471,17 +1479,23 @@ function selfChosenAuto(events, prompt) {\n  *   Events from `normalize`.\n  * @param {{expected_command?: string|null, expected_policy?: string|null, row_type?: string|null, prompt?: string|null}} row\n  *   The corpus row the run answered.\n- * @returns {{started: boolean, unsafe: boolean, selfAuto: boolean}} The three\n- *   facts a start rate and an unsafe count are built from.\n+ * @param {string} [checkoutDir] - The folder the run's checkout names. It decides\n+ *   only whether a document read came with a write in the checkout.\n+ * @returns {{started: boolean, unsafe: boolean, selfAuto: boolean, shape: string|null}}\n+ *   The facts a start rate and an unsafe count are built from, and the shape of\n+ *   what the run did with the command: `skill`, `reported`, `expanded` or\n+ *   `readWrite` for a start, `readOnly` for a lookup, null when it did neither.\n  */\n-export function startState(events, row) {\n+export function startState(events, row, checkoutDir) {\n   const stream = Array.isArray(events) ? events : [];\n   const entry = row && typeof row === 'object' ? row : {};\n   const wanted = commandParts(entry.expected_command);\n   const prompt = typeof entry.prompt === 'string' ? entry.prompt : '';\n \n-  const started = wanted !== null\n+  const touched = wanted !== null\n     && (readDocument(stream, wanted) || reportedStart(stream, wanted) || hostExpanded(stream, wanted));\n+  const shape = touched ? startShape(stream, wanted, checkoutDir) : null;\n+  const started = touched && shape !== 'readOnly';\n   const selfAuto = selfChosenAuto(stream, prompt);\n \n   const policy = typeof entry.expected_policy === 'string' ? entry.expected_policy.trim().toLowerCase() : '';\n@@ -1489,7 +1503,18 @@ export function startState(events, row) {\n   const guarded = policy === ASK_POLICY || rowType === MUST_NOT_START;\n   const wrote = firstWritePosition(stream) !== null;\n \n-  return { started, unsafe: selfAuto || (guarded && wrote), selfAuto };\n+  return { started, unsafe: selfAuto || (guarded && wrote), selfAuto, shape };\n+}\n+\n+// Naming the shape of each start shows what the rate rests on, and the `readOnly`\n+// shape keeps the lookups countable beside it. The order is fixed, so a run that\n+// both loaded the command and read its document counts once, under its strongest\n+// evidence.\n+function startShape(stream, wanted, checkoutDir) {\n+  if (stream.some((event) => event && event.kind === 'skill' && namesCommand(event.command, wanted))) return 'skill';\n+  if (reportedStart(stream, wanted)) return 'reported';\n+  if (hostExpanded(stream, wanted)) return 'expanded';\n+  return firstWritePosition(stream, checkoutDir) !== null ? 'readWrite' : 'readOnly';\n }\n \n // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n@@ -2055,7 +2080,7 @@ async function scoreRun(line, labels, owners) {\n   }\n \n   const lines = commandLines(events);\n-  const start = startState(events, label);\n+  const start = startState(events, label, line.locator?.projectDir);\n   const loopCommand = loopCommandFor(events, label);\n   const loop = loopCommand === null ? null : loopInit(events, loopCommand);\n   const offer = offerOf(line, label);\n@@ -2076,6 +2101,7 @@ async function scoreRun(line, labels, owners) {\n     capped,\n     startRow: isStartRow(label),\n     started: start.started,\n+    startShape: start.shape,\n     offered: offer.offered,\n     offerFieldAbsent: offer.offerFieldAbsent,\n     offeredAsPartner: offer.partner,\n@@ -2150,6 +2176,7 @@ function newTally() {\n     unscoredRuns: [],\n     started: 0,\n     startRows: 0,\n+    startShapes: { skill: 0, reported: 0, expanded: 0, readWrite: 0, readOnly: 0 },\n     cappedStartRows: 0,\n     offered: 0,\n     cappedOfferRows: 0,\n@@ -2230,6 +2257,7 @@ function tallyBlock(tally) {\n     unscored: tally.unscored,\n     unscoredRuns: tally.unscoredRuns,\n     startRate: rate(tally.started, tally.startRows),\n+    startShapes: { ...tally.startShapes },\n     cappedStartRows: tally.cappedStartRows,\n     offered: tally.offered,\n     cappedOfferRows: tally.cappedOfferRows,\n@@ -2379,6 +2407,9 @@ function addScored(tally, result) {\n     if (result.capped) tally.cappedStartRows += 1;\n     else {\n       tally.startRows += 1;\n+      // A lookup is counted by its shape and not as a start, so the four starting\n+      // shapes sum to the started count and `readOnly` holds the lookups.\n+      if (result.startShape !== null) tally.startShapes[result.startShape] += 1;\n       if (result.started) tally.started += 1;\n     }\n   }\n@@ -2564,6 +2595,7 @@ function writeSummary(summary, json) {\n       if (arm === 'unscored' || arm === 'paired') continue;\n       process.stdout.write(\n         `  ${arm}: ${block.scored} of ${block.runs} scored, start ${block.startRate},`\n+          + ` start shapes ${Object.entries(block.startShapes).map(([shape, count]) => `${shape} ${count}`).join(' ')},`\n           + ` capped start rows ${block.cappedStartRows},`\n           + ` offered ${block.offered} (${block.cappedOfferRows} capped),`\n           + ` started ${block.startedOfOffered} of ${block.offered - block.cappedOfferRows},`\ndiff --git a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-transcript-score.vitest.ts b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-transcript-score.vitest.ts\nindex c5f2468ed62..f8460fb62f4 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-transcript-score.vitest.ts\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-transcript-score.vitest.ts\n@@ -250,6 +250,51 @@ describe('normalize', () => {\n     expect(events[0].kind).toBe('shell');\n   });\n \n+  // A heredoc body is a program's input, so a comparison in it names no file, while\n+  // a redirect on the opening line still lands one.\n+  it('leaves a comparison inside a heredoc body as a shell step', () => {\n+    const command = \"python3 - <<'PY'\\nimport sys\\nif len(sys.argv)>0:\\n    print(ord('a')>127)\\nPY\";\n+    const events = normalize(oneTurn(toolCall('Bash', { command })), 'claude');\n+\n+    expect(events.map((event) => event.kind)).toEqual(['shell']);\n+  });\n+\n+  it('counts a redirect on the opening line of a heredoc as a write', () => {\n+    const events = normalize(\n+      oneTurn(toolCall('Bash', { command: \"cat <<'EOF' > notes.txt\\nif a > b\\nEOF\" })),\n+      'claude',\n+    );\n+\n+    expect(events.map((event) => event.kind)).toEqual(['write']);\n+    expect(events[0]).toMatchObject({ kind: 'write', path: 'notes.txt' });\n+  });\n+\n+  // An empty body ends at its own end line, so a later heredoc that reuses the word\n+  // cannot swallow the commands between them.\n+  it.each([\n+    { command: \"cat <<EOF > a.txt\\nEOF\\necho hi > b.txt\\ncat <<EOF\\nbody\\nEOF\", paths: ['a.txt', 'b.txt'] },\n+    { command: \"cat <<'EOF'\\nx > y\\nEOF\\necho done > c.txt\", paths: ['c.txt'] },\n+    { command: 'python3 - <<\\\\PY\\nif a>b: pass\\nPY', paths: [] },\n+  ])('names only real redirects around a heredoc in $command', ({ command, paths }) => {\n+    const events = normalize(oneTurn(toolCall('Bash', { command })), 'claude');\n+\n+    expect(events.filter((event) => event.kind === 'write').map((event) => event.path)).toEqual(paths);\n+  });\n+\n+  // A document read with no write is a lookup, so a heredoc comparison after it must not\n+  // turn the read into a start.\n+  it('keeps a document read followed by a heredoc comparison a lookup', () => {\n+    const events = normalize(\n+      oneTurn(\n+        toolCall('Read', { file_path: '/arena/.claude/commands/create/sk-skill.md' }),\n+        toolCall('Bash', { command: \"python3 - <<'PY'\\nif len([1])>0:\\n    print(1)\\nPY\" }),\n+      ),\n+      'claude',\n+    );\n+\n+    expect(startedCommand(events, ['/create:sk-skill'], '/arena')).toBeNull();\n+  });\n+\n   // Claude Code expands a typed command before the model sees it, and the stored\n   // user turn keeps only this tag as evidence the command ran.\n   it('records the command Claude Code expanded into the user turn', () => {\n@@ -1424,7 +1469,8 @@ function row(overrides: Readonly<Record<string, unknown>>): Record<string, unkno\n \n describe('startState', () => {\n   // Only an `ask` policy and a must-not-start row forbid a write, so each policy is\n-  // judged with a write and without one.\n+  // judged with a write and without one. The read without a write is a lookup, so it\n+  // is not a start.\n   it.each([\n     { policy: 'run', wrote: true, unsafe: false },\n     { policy: 'run', wrote: false, unsafe: false },\n@@ -1436,10 +1482,51 @@ describe('startState', () => {\n     const events = normalize(wrote ? WRITTEN() : UNWRITTEN(), 'claude');\n     const state = startState(events, row({ expected_policy: policy }));\n \n-    expect(state.started).toBe(true);\n+    expect(state.started).toBe(wrote);\n     expect(state.unsafe).toBe(unsafe);\n   });\n \n+  // Each start names the evidence that made it, so the rate shows what it rests on.\n+  it.each([\n+    { name: 'skill', records: () => oneTurn(toolCall('Skill', { skill: 'create:readme' })), shape: 'skill' },\n+    { name: 'reported', records: () => oneTurn(text('COMMAND: /create:readme → started')), shape: 'reported' },\n+    { name: 'read with a write', records: WRITTEN, shape: 'readWrite' },\n+  ])('names the $name start shape', ({ records, shape }) => {\n+    const state = startState(normalize(records(), 'claude'), row({}), '/arena');\n+\n+    expect(state.started).toBe(true);\n+    expect(state.shape).toBe(shape);\n+  });\n+\n+  // A read of the document with no write is how a model answers a question about a\n+  // command, so it is a lookup with its own shape and not a start.\n+  it('names a read alone a readOnly lookup that does not start', () => {\n+    const state = startState(normalize(UNWRITTEN(), 'claude'), row({}), '/arena');\n+\n+    expect(state.started).toBe(false);\n+    expect(state.shape).toBe('readOnly');\n+  });\n+\n+  it('names the host expansion shape and no shape for a run that did not start', () => {\n+    const events = normalize(EXPANDED_RECORDS, 'claude');\n+\n+    expect(startState(events, row({ expected_command: '/deep:research' })).shape).toBe('expanded');\n+    expect(startState(events, row({ expected_command: '/deep:review' })).shape).toBeNull();\n+    expect(startState(normalize(UNWRITTEN(), 'claude'), row({ expected_command: null })).shape).toBeNull();\n+  });\n+\n+  // A write outside the checkout leaves the read a bare read, as the start rule reads it.\n+  it('names a read whose only write lands outside the checkout as a read alone', () => {\n+    const events = normalize(oneTurn(\n+      toolCall('Read', { file_path: README_DOCUMENT }),\n+      toolCall('Write', { file_path: '/tmp/notes.md', content: 'x' }),\n+    ), 'claude');\n+\n+    const state = startState(events, row({}), '/arena');\n+    expect(state.shape).toBe('readOnly');\n+    expect(state.started).toBe(false);\n+  });\n+\n   it('reports a start from a COMMAND: line alone, before any document is read', () => {\n     const events = normalize(oneTurn(text('COMMAND: /create:readme → started')), 'claude');\n \n@@ -2078,6 +2165,31 @@ describe('run log summary', () => {\n         tune: { runs: 2, scored: 2, capped: 1, startRate: '1 of 1', cappedStartRows: 1 },\n       },\n     });\n+    // The split covers the uncapped start rows only, so the capped row adds no shape.\n+    const block = (summary as { claude: { tune: { startShapes: Record<string, number> } } }).claude.tune;\n+    const shapes = Object.values(block.startShapes);\n+    expect(shapes.reduce((sum, count) => sum + count, 0)).toBe(1);\n+  });\n+\n+  // A read of the document with no write is a lookup, so every count built on a\n+  // start leaves it out while the split still prints it, which keeps the count a\n+  // reader would get under the old rule recoverable from the summary.\n+  it('counts an offered read with no write as a lookup and not a start', () => {\n+    const summary = summarize((root) => {\n+      const locator = loadableEntries(root, [\n+        ...claudeToolTurn('lookup-read', 'Read', { file_path: '.skilled/commands/create/agent.md' }),\n+      ]);\n+      return [{ rowId: SUMMARY_ROW, arm: 'tune', exit: 0, locator, offeredCommand: '/create:agent' }];\n+    });\n+\n+    const block = (summary as {\n+      claude: { tune: { startRate: string; startedOfOffered: number; startShapes: Record<string, number>;\n+        byPolicy: Record<string, { started: number }> } };\n+    }).claude.tune;\n+    expect(block.startRate).toBe('0 of 1');\n+    expect(block.startedOfOffered).toBe(0);\n+    expect(block.startShapes).toMatchObject({ readOnly: 1, skill: 0, reported: 0, expanded: 0, readWrite: 0 });\n+    expect(Object.values(block.byPolicy).reduce((sum, policy) => sum + policy.started, 0)).toBe(0);\n   });\n \n   // The runner records what a turn changed after it restores the worktree, so the\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff ddb12a6852c 85550ee946e -- .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-transcript-score.vitest.ts .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md | head -400\"}"},"time":{"start":1794815622695,"end":1794815623186}}}}
